# TRAPDOOR lab configuration
# Copy to .env and edit. .env is gitignored.

# Hostname the honeypots present to an attacker. Should look like a boring
# internal box, not "honeypot".
SENSOR_HOSTNAME=web01

# Identity of this sensor in the normalized output. Lets several lab sensors
# feed one index without ambiguity.
SENSOR_NAME=lab-sensor-01

# Interface IP the honeypot ports bind to. Default 0.0.0.0 is convenient;
# in a real lab set this to the LXC's lab-facing address so nothing else
# on the host can reach it.
LAB_BIND_IP=0.0.0.0

# Only this CIDR may be allowed through the host firewall. Written by
# `make firewall` — keep it to the lab subnet.
LAB_CIDR=10.20.30.0/24

# Published ports
COWRIE_SSH_PORT=2222
COWRIE_TELNET_PORT=2223
WEB_PORT=8080

# The console: the human view of what the honeypots caught. Read-only.
# 8081 on purpose, because 8080 is the decoy and must stay bait.
CONSOLE_PORT=8081

# Who may read the console. DELIBERATELY NOT LAB_CIDR.
#
# The decoy is meant to be reachable, so LAB_CIDR is right for it. The console
# is the opposite: it shows every attack every student threw, including their
# source addresses, the usernames and passwords they guessed, and the commands
# they typed. Scoped to a whole lab subnet, one student can sit and watch every
# other student. Set this to the management subnet, in CIDR form.
#
# Left empty, firewall.sh derives it from this host's own address, so the
# default is never "the whole network". Set it explicitly for anything real.
#
# On a deployment that is ONE network (the whole IT department on a single
# /16, no student VLAN) the honest value here IS the /16. Narrowing it to a
# /24 to look safer locks out most of the people the console is for, which is
# the 2026-11-12 afternoon all over again. Narrow is not the virtue.
# CORRECT is, and correctness is declared below.
#
# LEFT COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE, and this is a bad default. Uncomment it and
# set it to YOUR operator network. While it is commented out, firewall.sh
# derives the scope from this host's own address, which on a flat farm is the
# whole farm. That is documented, it is not a bug, and it is still the state
# you are in if you copy this file and edit only what DEPLOY.md shows in bold.
# Jim flagged the mismatch on 2026-11-18: this file parks the variable, the
# deploy doc asks you to set it. The doc is right. Uncomment this.
# ADMIN_CIDR=192.168.111.0/24

# WHO IS ALLOWED TO READ WHAT STUDENTS TYPED, IN WRITING.
#
# Format: <cidr>|<who is on it, and why it is that wide>
#
# verify.sh check 8 refuses to pass without this. The check cannot ask "is the
# console narrow" on a network that has nowhere narrower to go; it can ask
# whether a human has said out loud who may read the console and whether the
# console agrees. An undeclared scope is an unowned scope.
#
# The reason clause is not decoration. A /16 reads as a mistake until someone
# writes down that the department is one flat network. That sentence is the
# difference between a boundary and an accident.
#
# Example for this farm:
# QUOTE THE VALUE. verify.sh loads this file with `source`, so an unquoted `|`
# is a shell pipe: on 2026-11-18 the unquoted form below ran `entire` as a
# command, destroyed the variable, and made check 8 report "NO operator network
# is declared" on a box where it WAS declared. The quotes are not style.
# CONSOLE_NETWORK_DECLARED='192.168.0.0/16|entire IT department, single network, no student VLAN'
CONSOLE_NETWORK_DECLARED=

# How long normalized events are kept, in days. Honeypot data grows fast and
# is mostly noise; short retention is the safe default.
RETENTION_DAYS=14
